\details{
PostgreSQL returns rows in no fixed order. The same query can return the
same rows in a different order on each call. Code that reads \code{d[1]},
\code{d[.N]} or \code{tail(d, 1)} after \code{dplyr::collect()} therefore gets an arbitrary
row. \code{collect_and_sort_by_pk()} makes that row deterministic.

\link{DBTable_v9}\verb{$tbl()} attaches the primary key of the table to the lazy table
as the attribute \code{csdb_pk}. These dbplyr verbs keep the attribute on their
result: \code{filter()}, \code{select()}, \code{mutate()}, \code{group_by()}, \code{summarise()},
\code{arrange()}, \code{distinct()}, \code{head()}, and \code{left_join()} and \code{inner_join()} on
the \code{x} side.

The function sorts with \code{\link[=setorder_by_pk]{setorder_by_pk()}}. The primary key columns that
the result has come first, in primary key order. Every other column follows,
in column order. A primary key column that a \code{select()} or a \code{summarise()}
removed is not a sort column.

The attribute holds column names. After a \code{rename()} or a \code{mutate()} of a
primary key column, the order is still fixed, but it is no longer the
primary key order.

The sort runs after the collect. It cannot choose which rows a \code{head()} or
another limit inside the query returns. Put a \code{dplyr::arrange()} before the
limit when the rows matter.

The function stops with an error when \code{x} carries no primary key. It does
not fall back to an unsorted collect. These are the causes:
\itemize{
\item the query did not start at \link{DBTable_v9}\verb{$tbl()}.
\item the table has no primary key, because its \code{keys} is \code{NULL}.
\item a step dropped the attribute, for example \code{\link[dplyr:compute]{dplyr::compute()}}, or a join
where the csdb table is on the \code{y} side.
}

For a result without a primary key, for example from \code{\link[DBI:dbGetQuery]{DBI::dbGetQuery()}},
use \code{\link[=setorder_by_pk]{setorder_by_pk()}} with \code{table = NULL}.
}
